<item>
  <title>Millburn Road - The Data</title>
  <link>https://themodeshift.scot/posts/2026-09-04-inverness-data/</link>
  <description><![CDATA[ 




<p>To understand more about the bike lane on Millburn Road, I wanted to look at the cycling traffic levels right across the city of Inverness. The excellent news is that <a href="https://cycling-scotland-dashboard.usmart.io/">Cycling Scotland have an Open Data portal</a> that tracks lots of places across Scotland. The less great news is that the data starts in 2021, so there’s no simple before/after picture to see the effect of the installation of new bike lanes during the COVID-19 pandemic. There’s still a lot to learn from what is there though!</p>
<p>Millburn Road has two traffic counters, one each for the Eastward and Westward sections of the road. The point where the counters appear on the map is almost exactly where the section of bike lane has been removed making it a good place to measure bike traffic:</p>
<div class="quarto-figure quarto-figure-center">
<figure class="figure">
<p><img src="https://themodeshift.scot/posts/2026-09-04-inverness-data/usmart-screenshot-millburn-west.png" class="img-fluid figure-img" alt="A map zoomed in on Millburn Road in Inverness on which two traffic counters are marked. The right of the image shows some of the stats from the counter."></p>
<figcaption>Image taken from Cycling Scotland Open Data portal (usmart.io)</figcaption>
</figure>
</div>
<p>By grabbing the data from both the <a href="https://umove.usmart.ai/organisation/peopleandplace/dashboard/cycle-open-data?site=HLD002">Eastward</a> and <a href="https://umove.usmart.ai/organisation/peopleandplace/dashboard/cycle-open-data?site=HLD011">Westward</a> counters it’s pretty easy to see which side of the road has a bike lane:</p>
